Setting. A totally ordered set L is cut into strata: 'vertices' (points, lowercase x) and 'open intervals' (capital X). A finite configuration 𝔏, called a '(μ̂-)polygone', is a set of such strata. The relation x ◁ X means that x is an endpoint (a face) of the interval X. Z ≪ X is the specialisation/incidence order (Z lies in the closure of X, or is X). The previous page defined:
- the complementary polygon 𝔏* = 𝔏̂ \ 𝔏;
- the redundant vertices 𝔏_red = {s ∈ 𝔏_0 | ord(s,𝔏) = 2}, i.e. interior vertices of 𝔏 adjacent on both sides to intervals of 𝔏;
- the bi-complement 𝔏**, whose support is ∂𝔏 \ 𝔏_red.

This page describes 𝔏** combinatorially. It is not obtained by simply erasing the redundant vertices. It is obtained by composing (concatenating) the intervals adjacent to them:
- (𝔏**)_0 = 𝔏_0 \ 𝔏_red;
- (𝔏**)_1 = the intervals obtained by merging each maximal convex chain X_1 < x_1 < X_2 < … < x_{n-1} < X_n in 𝔏, with x_i ◁ X_i, X_{i+1}, into a single interval.
These merged intervals correspond 1-1 to the convex components of 𝔏. A convex component is such a chain, possibly enlarged by an end vertex x_0 ◁ X_1, by x_n ◁ X_n, or by both.

Next he defines the 'shadow' (ombre) of 𝔏 in M as the down-closure: Omb°_M(𝔏) = ∪_{X∈𝔏} Omb°_M(X) = {Z ∈ M | ∃X ∈ 𝔏, Z ≪ X}. This prepares the 'key result' on the next page. For a nonempty M̂-presheaf Φ: cos-Ψ°_M(Φ) = cos-Ψ°(Omb°_M(Φ)) = Omb°_M(Φ*). Under a hypothesis H_Φ: s-Ψ°_M(Φ) = Omb°_M(Φ**).

The purpose seems to be to compute some complement or closure operator Ψ on down-closed sets of strata of a one-dimensional stratified ordered set. This would feed into a study of flags Drap(L) and the 'ateliers' of chapter IX. In effect it is a duality/regularisation statement in the spirit of 'interior of closure' for subdivided lines: the double complement merges intervals across removable vertices.
